  place, sub-project 3 has to retrofit an event log or diff snapshots, which is the expensive version. Designing tasks
  so mutations produce a change record is cheap now and nearly free later. I'll carry that into the design for #1 rather
  than treating it as a notifications problem.

  Starting on sub-project 1. First question — this one shapes everything downstream, so the comparison first:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether tasks live in the browser or on a server. This decides whether sub-project 2 (identity) is even
  possible as specified, and it's the single most expensive thing to change later — it determines the language, the
  deploy story, and whether "notify a user who isn't looking at the page" can exist at all.

  - [1mServer-backed (API + database)[0m — real multi-user, real notifications to people who aren't watching. Costs a backend,
    a deploy target, and more setup before the first visible thing works.
  - [1mClient-only (browser storage)[0m — fastest to something on screen, zero infra. But it rules out sub-project 2 as a real
    feature and caps sub-project 3 at "notify the one person in this tab." Reversing this means rewriting the data
    layer.
  - [1mLocal-first with sync later[0m — client-side store designed against an interface a server can back later. Buys early
    velocity without fully closing the door, at the cost of building the abstraction twice-ish.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m server-backed. You described a multi-user outcome — "users get notified when tasks [3mthey[0m care about
  change" — and the other two options quietly redefine that goal into something smaller. Given the endpoint is
  notifications, paying for the backend now is the cheaper path.
